WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.492
NOT IN can be surprisingly tricky when NULL appears inside the list.

00:00:04.492 --> 00:00:10.392
Conceptually, NOT IN asks whether the value is different from every item in the list.

00:00:10.392 --> 00:00:17.242
If the list contains a NULL, SQL cannot prove that the value is different from that unknown item.

00:00:17.242 --> 00:00:19.852
The result can become UNKNOWN.

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:26.402
Because WHERE keeps only TRUE, rows that look like obvious non-matches may disappear.

00:00:26.402 --> 00:00:32.182
For example, suppose we ask for payment statuses NOT IN Paid, Closed, and NULL.

00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:33.822
You may expect Open to qualify.

00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:43.402
But the presence of NULL makes the logic uncertain because SQL cannot compare Open with an unknown value in a

00:00:43.402 --> 00:00:44.587
definite way.

00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:52.202
The safe strategy is usually to keep NULL out of the IN or NOT IN list and handle missingness explicitly.

00:00:52.202 --> 00:01:00.172
If your rule is “status is not Paid, including missing status,” write a NULL-aware condition that says exactly

00:01:00.172 --> 00:01:06.272
that. If your rule is “status is known and is not Paid,” then require IS NOT NULL along with the inequality.

00:01:06.272 --> 00:01:12.552
This is another case where the business policy matters more than the keyword.

00:01:12.552 --> 00:01:17.872
Whenever you see NOT IN in production SQL, especially against a subquery,

00:01:17.872 --> 00:01:21.002
ask whether the compared values can contain NULL.

00:01:21.002 --> 00:01:23.291
If they can, test carefully.
